WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:11.604
This video shows how to control a pre-rendered SVG with no library at all. Its CSS animation plays and pauses with two classes, and any script can set them.

2
00:00:12.133 --> 00:00:16.182
First, let's put the file into the page, so a script can reach it.

3
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:34.639
The markup of the file goes into the ball element. Nothing moves yet, because the animation is not enabled.

4
00:00:35.867 --> 00:00:40.412
Now the Play button. It adds two classes to the file's root.

5
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.783
One class turns the animation on, and the other lets it run.

6
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.202
Let's press Play.

7
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.943
Both classes are on, so it plays.

8
00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:07.745
The Pause button removes only the playing class. Let's add it.

9
00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:20.067
The animation stays on, but it stops where it is.

10
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.834
Let's press Play, and then Pause.

11
00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:29.417
It stops right where it was.

12
00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:33.801
The Reset button removes both classes. Let's add it.

13
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:50.556
Without the enabled class, the animation is off, and the file shows its first frame.

14
00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.203
Let's press Play, and then Reset.

15
00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.693
It jumps back to the first frame.

16
00:02:00.933 --> 00:02:10.781
So, a pre-rendered SVG needs no library. Two classes on its root play it, pause it, and reset it, from any script or framework.
